Veloso wrote:

[changes chameleon stone to chameleon potion]

> I'm not sure I like stack dipping only affecting a
> single dipped item.  It's inconsistent with !oPoly
> and !oHolyWater,

So make dipping a stack into a stack work as it does
for the current cases: affects all of the dipped
items the same way (clones each one in an identical
manner for all of them), consumes _one_ of the
dipped-into chameleon potions.

> and dipping a stack of +3 darts into a stack of
> potions of duplication generates two new

[stacks of]

> objects (a +1 dart and a +2 dart)

[a stack of +1 darts and a stack of +2 darts]

> that won't stack in your inventory, so now two

[stacks of]

> items drop to the floor.

If your chameleon potion is as rare as previously
indicated, a _stack_ of chameleon potions is going
to be so rare that the player can just learn to put
up with the inconvenience in the sometimes case that
there are also no open inventory slots (and anyway,
would like both cloned items or stacks to drop, so
as to s/be able to pick up the more desired one).

>  But I can't think of another consistent way to
>  handle this case,

So make it work "as expected".

> and being able to double a large stack in size is
> probably broken.

Not really. It is like many other game features, a
short cut to a more tedious way of doing the same
thing. In the case of doubling stack of daggers,
mines camping or altar camping or pudding farming
are all other, slower ways to do the same thing, so
you haven't much affected game balance by providing
a way to double stacks.

    There is an interesting question, though, about
    how to handle the results of doubling a stack of
    _gems_, since they can figure so prominently in
    a mid-level player's final game score.

> (And maybe two objects dropping to the floor isn't
> such a terrible outcome.

Agreed.

> I only made chameleon stones not stack for the
> cool effect of a stone keeping the same inventory
> letter once it did its cloning magic.  But that's
> not needed here.)

Well, it would still be nice, and except for the
very rare case of multiple same-B/U/Ced chameleon
potions, would still happen.

I missed out, can you _wish_ for a chameleon potion?

Arguably, compared to wishing up an enchanted stack
of runed daggers, wishing for a chameleon stone and
cloning an existing enchanted stack of runed daggers
might be even more powerful, and something one would
need spoiling to realize, much like knowing to make
